//? Title: Minimum-area bounding rectangles
//? Subtitle: Actual hull-edge candidates and the original rotated rectangle
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find a rectangle of smallest area containing a planar point set while allowing
its orientation to vary. We derive hull-edge projection candidates, inspect
their actual extents, retain the original rotated6-by2 rectangle, and check
rotation behavior. The native implementation reprojects the hull at every
orientation, so its scan is quadratic in hull size rather than the linear
rotating-calipers walk.
)

\toc

> Reduce the search to hull-edge orientations

Interior points cannot change a supporting line, so first compute the convex
hull. A minimum-area enclosing rectangle of a nondegenerate polygon has a side
aligned with a hull edge. For an outgoing unit edge u and its perpendicular v,
project hull points relative to its origin. The enclosing rectangle in that
orientation has widths equal to the two projection ranges.

$$
A(u)=(\max_p u\cdot(p-a)-\min_p u\cdot(p-a))(\max_p v\cdot(p-a)-\min_p v\cdot(p-a)).
$$
\alttext(Candidate area is the product of the parallel and perpendicular projection ranges.)

Testing all hull edges therefore suffices in exact geometry. The native hull
uses exact-sign orientation predicates; projected extents, areas and
reconstructed corners use floating-point arithmetic. The orientation theorem
does not turn floating rounding into an exact area certificate. The producer
retains the first strict area improvement, so mathematically tied orientations
can differ slightly numerically.

> A small right triangle

For points(0,0),(3,0),(0,2), the horizontal candidate has width3, height2 and
area6. Other hull orientations may tie. The diagram and table show the actual
first candidate extents and actual selection decisions, using the canonical
producer's own hull order.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rectangle_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::geometry::minimum_bounding_rectangle_trace({{0, 0}, {3, 0}, {0, 2}});
    auto &c = t.candidates.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("u", {0, -2},
           std::format("Actual parallel extent\n{} to {}", c.min_u, c.max_u)).node("v", {0, 2}, std::format("Actual perpendicular extent\n{} to {}", c.min_v, c.max_v)).node("a", {8, 0}, std::format("Actual candidate area\n{}", c.area)).edge("u", "a").edge("v", "a");
    std::print("| hull edge | min u | max u | min v | max v | actual area | strict improvement |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &r : t.candidates)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.edge, r.min_u,
                   r.max_u, r.min_v, r.max_v, r.area, r.selected);
    d.save_svg("build/rectangle_tiny.svg");
}
```
