> Retain the original rotated6-by2 rectangle

The original four corners are generated by rotating an origin-anchored6-by2
rectangle through30degrees. Its true area is12. The axis-aligned box has
area12+40 sin(theta)cos(theta), approximately29.3205 here; it includes extra
empty space. The next plot overlays both boxes in equal-scale geometry. Thin
report loops only map returned coordinates into plot series; the generator and
rectangle algorithm live in the library.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rectangle_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto points = dm::geometry::bounding_rectangle_demo();
    auto r = dm::geometry::minimum_bounding_rectangle(points);
    auto b = dm::geometry::bounding_box(points);
    std::vector<double> x, y, rx, ry;
    for (auto q : points) {
        x.push_back(q.x);
        y.push_back(q.y);
    }
    for (auto q : r.corners) {
        rx.push_back(q.x);
        ry.push_back(q.y);
    }
    rx.push_back(rx.front());
    ry.push_back(ry.front());
    std::print("| box | actual area |\n| --- | ---: |\n| oriented | {} |\n| axis aligned | {} |\n",
               r.area, (b.max.x - b.min.x) * (b.max.y - b.min.y));
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line({b.min.x, b.max.x, b.max.x, b.min.x, b.min.x},
           {b.min.y, b.min.y, b.max.y, b.max.y, b.min.y}, "axis-aligned box",
           {107, 114, 128}, 2).line(rx, ry, "native oriented box",
                                    {220, 38, 38}, 2).points(x, y,
                                                             "original corners",
                                                             {37, 99, 235}, 7).size(850, 850).margins(95, 35, 60, 70).x_limits(-2, 7).y_limits(-2, 7).title("Original rotated rectangle: oriented versus axis-aligned").x_label("x").y_label("y").save_svg("build/rectangle_original.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| box | actual area |
| --- | ---: |
| oriented | 12 |
| axis aligned | 29.32050807568877 |
\image(figures/rectangle_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original rotated rectangle and its two enclosing boxes.)
\alttext(The oriented area is approximately12 and the axis-aligned area approximately29.3205, with equal geometric scales.)

> Inspect the actual orientation scan

Each hull edge causes a complete pass over all hull vertices. For a four-vertex
rectangle, the producer executes sixteen point projections and four candidate
decisions. Tiny area differences reflect floating rounding among tied exact
solutions. A selected flag means improvement at that moment, not that every
selected candidate is the final answer.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rectangle_candidates.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::geometry::minimum_bounding_rectangle_trace(dm::geometry::bounding_rectangle_demo());
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("Actual projections: {}. Capture units: {}.\n\n| actual hull edge | candidate area | strict " "improvement |\n| ---: | ---: | --- |\n",
               t.projections, t.captured_entries);
    for (auto &r : t.candidates) {
        x.push_back(r.edge);
        y.push_back(r.area);
        std::print("| {} | {:.17g} | {} |\n", r.edge, r.area, r.selected);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual candidate area", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(0, 15).title("Original rectangle: actual orientation candidates").x_label("hull edge index").y_label("candidate area").save_svg("build/rectangle_candidates.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Actual projections: 16. Capture units: 16.

| actual hull edge | candidate area | strict improvement |
| ---: | ---: | --- |
| 0 | 12 | true |
| 1 | 12 | false |
| 2 | 12.000000000000005 | false |
| 3 | 12.000000000000004 | false |
\image(figures/rectangle_candidates.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual candidate areas, displayed with a common zero-based scale.)
\alttext(Four equivalent exact orientations yield approximately12; exact printed digits expose rounding differences.)

> Check rotation behavior and state the cost

Across the finite angle sweep below, the oriented area should remain12 to
rounding, while the axis-aligned area changes with rotation. This checks a known
geometric family rather than inferring a universal optimum from an image. No
external dataset is necessary for this geometry kernel.

With n input points and h hull vertices, canonical hull construction costs O(n
log n), followed by h squared point projections. The scan is O(h squared), not
the O(h) support-pointer version of rotating calipers. Driver and captured
history are O(n+h). Trace quota units are original points, hull points,
candidate records and four final corners; default1000000, zero disables. Invalid
nonfinite coordinates or a hull with fewer than three vertices throw. Finite but
unrepresentable projection geometry, area or corners also throws.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/rectangle_rotation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, oriented, aligned;
    std::print("| angle degrees | native oriented area | native axis-aligned area |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    for (int degree = 0; degree <= 90; degree += 5) {
        auto points =
            dm::geometry::bounding_rectangle_demo(6, 2,
                                                  degree * std::numbers::pi /
                                                  180);
        auto r = dm::geometry::minimum_bounding_rectangle(points);
        auto b = dm::geometry::bounding_box(points);
        double a = (b.max.x - b.min.x) * (b.max.y - b.min.y);
        x.push_back(degree);
        oriented.push_back(r.area);
        aligned.push_back(a);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", degree, r.area, a);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, oriented, "native oriented area", {37, 99, 235}, 2).line(
        x, aligned, "native axis-aligned area", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title(
        "Rotation: oriented and axis-aligned areas").x_label("rotation degrees").y_label("area").save_svg("build/rectangle_rotation.svg");
}
```
